<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kaga Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1583 CE – 1871 CE · capital Kanazawa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The richest domain of Tokugawa Japan, held by the Maeda family at Kanazawa and famed as the million-koku domain of Kaga.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-toshiie">Maeda Toshiie</a>, a comrade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, received northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaga-province">Kaga</a> after the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibata-katsuie">Shibata Katsuie</a> in 1583 and moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a> castle. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-toshinaga">Toshinaga</a> sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> in 1600 and was rewarded with lands covering Kaga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noto-province">Noto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etchu-province">Etchu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Assessed at over a million koku, the domain was second only to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>'s own holdings. As outside (tozama) lords the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-clan">Maeda</a> were watched carefully, and they spent heavily on culture rather than armies to reassure the shogunate. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-tsunanori">Maeda Tsunanori</a> patronised scholarship and crafts, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a> became a centre of gold leaf, Kutani <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaga-province">Kaga</a> yuzen dyeing, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenroku-en">Kenroku-en</a> garden.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The domain stayed cautious during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> and joined the new government. It was abolished in 1871, becoming the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishikawa-prefecture">Ishikawa Prefecture</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a> remains a cultural centre.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kaga-domain.jpg" alt="Kaga Domain" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Chris Gladis from Kyoto, Japan · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kanazawa_Castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1583 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kanazawa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maeda Toshiie, Maeda Toshitsune, Maeda Tsunanori</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Jodo Shinshu Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kanazawa Domain, Kaga han</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1727&amp;focus=kaga-domain" aria-label="Kaga Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8114 859 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kaga Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8434 1115L8440 1178M8434 1115L8530 1145M8434 1115L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8440 1178h0M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8434 1115h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8434 1115h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kanazawa. <a href="/atlas?year=1727&amp;focus=kaga-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
